<section class="shell band faq">
  <h2>Questions about the methods</h2>

  <details>
    <summary>Which method will Reem use with me?</summary>
    <div class="answer-body">
      <p>Whichever the week calls for. She is a NARM therapist, a Somatic Experiencing
      Practitioner and EMDR certified, so the approaches move with what you bring rather than
      being chosen in advance. NARM tracks the pattern, Somatic Experiencing sets the pacing,
      IFS-informed EMDR handles processing when there is room for it.</p>
    </div>
  </details>

  <details>
    <summary>Do the methods get used in the same session?</summary>
    <div class="answer-body">
      <p>Often, yes. They are not sequential modules. A single session can start with noticing
      what the body is doing, name the pattern that showed up, and end without processing
      anything at all if that is what the day holds.</p>
    </div>
  </details>

  <details>
    <summary>Why not just do standard EMDR?</summary>
    <div class="answer-body">
      <p>For a single discrete trauma with a stable nervous system, standard protocol often works
      well. When trauma is developmental and layered, parts of you have organized around keeping
      it away, and processing that skips those parts tends to flood, stall or stop working.
      Consulting them first is slower at the start and faster overall.</p>
    </div>
  </details>

  <details>
    <summary>What is Brainspotting, and where does it fit?</summary>
    <div class="answer-body">
      <p>An approach that uses fixed eye position to access where material is held, developed out
      of EMDR practice. Here it is an alternate route rather than a main one: useful when EMDR
      targeting will not settle, or when a client processes more easily without the back-and-forth
      of bilateral stimulation.</p>
    </div>
  </details>
